      "problem": "What are the coordinates of the minimum point of the function $y=(x-1)^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ is located at $(h,0).$\n\nFor the given function $y=(x-1)^{2},$ we have $h=1.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $1$ unit *right*. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14241402/assets/9038cd5affa57a94334ab4af4d8a41bd1702f8f2664c0ae5b30ff9e74b6bfe90.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(1,0),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point.",
